(; expect dk=1 complete=0 ;)
(; Dedukti (0b67dab) does not match a pattern variable that occurs at least twice under the
   pattern's binders when its instance mentions a variable of the redex's own context.

   Here F occurs twice under three binders, and in the assertion both occurrences stand for
   `d q`, where q is bound outside the redex.  The left-hand side instantiated with F := d q
   is the redex, so the declared rule rewrites it to ok: by the matching relation of
   lambda-Pi modulo rewriting, d q matches F (its free variable q is not one of the pattern's
   binders) and the two occurrences match equal terms.

   dk_tcalc (complete matching, the default) fires the rule and accepts the assertion.
   dk check refuses it, and so does dk_tcalc --dk-matching.  Dedukti does fire the rule when
   the instance is closed, when F occurs once, and when at most one occurrence is under a
   binder (repeated_var_matches_once_under.dk). ;)

A : Type.
a : A.
ok : A.
c : A -> A -> A.
d : A -> A.
def g3 : (A -> A -> A -> A) -> A.

[F] g3 (x => y => z => c F F) --> ok.

#ASSERT (q : A => g3 (u => v => w => c (d q) (d q))) == (q : A => ok).
